  "narrative": "H7 is now independently validated. Discovery R7 gave a signed estimate of +0.8019 [0.7823, 0.8216], and validation R92 gives +0.7904 [0.7615, 0.8194] at the validation alpha level. Because the claim direction is +1, the positive raw exposed-minus-comparator difference means TP53-loss lines have a less negative USP7 dependency score, i.e. weaker USP7 dependency. The validation interval is tight, excludes zero, and is extremely large relative to the 0.1 practical benchmark for dependency-score contrasts. Combined with the prior consistency across adenocarcinoma lines, Avana and non-Avana screens, high-quality screens, rejected library/screen-quality interactions, and the rejected broad Avana-library main effect, I accept H7 and close it as a validated dataset-level association. It is an association within this supplied dataset, not a causal claim or generalizable biological rule.\n\nH67 response deadline: independent validation R65 gives a signed estimate of +0.3288 [0.2553, 0.4022] for PTEN loss versus STAT3 dependency, compared with discovery R55 of +0.2674 [0.2153, 0.3195]. The claim direction is -1, so the underlying raw contrast is negative: PTEN-loss lines have a more negative STAT3 dependency score, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency. The validation interval excludes zero and the point estimate is well above the practical 0.1 benchmark. This is reinforced by the accepted TP53-wildtype context H96, the accepted TP53-loss context H97, and the rejected TP53-loss interaction H98. I therefore accept and close H67 as a validated dataset-level PTEN-STAT3 association, again only within this dataset and not as causal or generalizable.\n\nWith TP53-USP7, PTEN-STAT3, KEAP1-NFE2L2, SMARCA4-SMARCA2, MSI-high-WRN, and CDKN2A-MDM2 now validated or robustly supported, the next highest-value work is focused specificity and one underexplored pathway. I activate H95 to test whether TP53 loss also has a broad STAT3 association. Given that PTEN-STAT3 is not driven by a TP53 interaction and TP53-USP7 is highly selective, I expect H95 to be null, but it is a useful p53-pathway specificity test. I activate H104 to test PTEN-STAT3 within high-quality screens, completing a robustness check for the validated PTEN effect. I also activate H68, MYC amplification versus RPL11 dependency, and register a related new proposal, CDKN2A loss versus RPL11 dependency. RPL11 is biologically adjacent to MDM2 and p53 regulation; CDKN2A loss already predicts stronger MDM2 dependency, so testing RPL11 may reveal whether the CDKN2A association has p53-pathway specificity or extends to related ribosomal stress machinery. If these tests are null or below the practical benchmark, they will further sharpen the accepted set. If any meaningful effect emerges, it will require context checks before validation. All current conclusions remain dataset-level associations, not causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Major validated or strongly supported dataset-level associations: KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H13, validated); MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency (H8, validated, high-quality robustness H75, specificity null for DHX9/POLR2A); SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H10, validated, high-quality robustness H74); PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency (H67 validated; contexts H96 TP53-wt and H97 TP53-loss accepted; no TP53 interaction H98); CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency (H94 validated; contexts TP53 status, MTAP-intact, high-quality accepted; no TP53 interaction H101); and now TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency (H7 validated R92 after robustness H16/H44/H50/H73 and no broad Avana artifact H35). Many other targeted predictors/outcomes were null, supporting specificity.\n\nNext active follow-ups: H95 TP53 loss versus STAT3 as a p53-pathway specificity check; H104 PTEN loss versus STAT3 in high-quality screens; H68 MYC amplification versus RPL11; new CDKN2A loss versus RPL11 to test adjacent p53/MDM2/RPL11 biology. Prioritize interpretation: accepted associations are dataset-level associations only, not causal or generalizable. If H95 and RPL11 tests are null, the accepted set is complete and further broad exploration should focus on remaining untested mechanisms or context robustness. If RPL11 becomes meaningful, require context tests before requesting validation. Remaining validation requests should be reserved for newly meaningful unvalidated associations, not for already validated canonical effects."
